From: plastron
Subject: [PATCH] input: rocknix-singleadc-joypad: rumble that holds up on the GKD Pixel 2

Two fixes to the driver's rumble path (its has_rumble side, which the
GKD Pixel 2's device tree turns on by giving the joypad PWM0).

No mutex in rumble_play_effect(). ff-memless calls it with the input
device's event_lock held and interrupts off, from a write to the device
and from its own timer. Uncontended, mutex_lock() passed; held by the
play work (applying the PWM, which sleeps), it slept in atomic context,
and Crazy Taxi 2, which eases its rumble every frame, froze the device
within seconds (2026-11-05). The level is one u16, written whole, and the
work still reads it under the mutex.

The pin settled at probe. PWM0 comes up latched driving its pin high,
which runs the motor. The probe applies the off state with the device
tree's normal polarity, and the registers take it (the PWM debugfs reads
back "disabled, normal polarity"), but the pin stays high: read from the
pin through the GPIO block, it went low only once the channel had run, at
any duty. So the motor ran from the moment the pin became PWM0 until a
game first stopped a rumble. ROCKNIX's 030-pwm-vibra-kick-stuck-boot-state
did the same for pwm-vibrator with a ramp from 90% duty over 250 ms, felt
at every boot. This runs the channel once at 0% duty, low on the pin, for
three periods (3 ms at the device tree's 1 ms), then turns it off: the pin
reads low from then on, and there is nothing to feel.

---
 drivers/input/joystick/rocknix-singleadc-joypad.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/joystick/rocknix-singleadc-joypad.c b/drivers/input/joystick/rocknix-singleadc-joypad.c
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rocknix-singleadc-joypad.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rocknix-singleadc-joypad.c
@@ -777,21 +777,25 @@
 	if (effect->type != FF_RUMBLE)
 		 return 0;
 
-	mutex_lock(&joypad->lock);
-	if (!joypad->rumble_enabled) {
-		mutex_unlock(&joypad->lock);
+	/*
+	 * No mutex here: ff-memless calls this with the input device's
+	 * event_lock held and interrupts off, from a write to the device and
+	 * from its own timer. Uncontended, mutex_lock() passed; held by the
+	 * play work below (applying the PWM, which sleeps) it slept in atomic
+	 * context, and a game changing its rumble every frame froze the
+	 * GKD Pixel 2 within seconds. The level is one u16, written whole, and
+	 * the work reads it under the mutex as before.
+	 */
+	if (!READ_ONCE(joypad->rumble_enabled))
 		return 0; // Ignore rumble effects if disabled
-	}
 
 	if (effect->u.rumble.strong_magnitude)
 		boosted_level = effect->u.rumble.strong_magnitude + joypad->boost_strong;
 	else
 		boosted_level = effect->u.rumble.weak_magnitude + joypad->boost_weak;
 
-	joypad->level = (u16)CLAMP(boosted_level, 0, 0xffff);
+	WRITE_ONCE(joypad->level, (u16)CLAMP(boosted_level, 0, 0xffff));
 
-	mutex_unlock(&joypad->lock);
-
 	schedule_work(&joypad->play_work);
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -825,6 +829,29 @@
 		 return error;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * On the GKD Pixel 2 (PX30S) the channel comes up latched driving its
+	 * pin high, which runs the motor, and the off state applied above
+	 * changes the registers but not the pin: read back from the pin, it
+	 * stays high until the channel has run once. So run it once, at 0%
+	 * duty (low on the pin, nothing to feel), for three of its periods,
+	 * and turn it off again.
+	 */
+	state.duty_cycle = 0;
+	state.enabled = true;
+	error = pwm_apply_might_sleep(joypad->pwm, &state);
+	if (!error) {
+		unsigned long us = (unsigned long)div_u64(state.period, 1000) * 3;
+
+		usleep_range(us, us + 1000);
+		state.enabled = false;
+		error = pwm_apply_might_sleep(joypad->pwm, &state);
+	}
+	if (error) {
+		dev_err(dev, "failed to settle the PWM's pin: %d", error);
+		return error;
+	}
+
 	dev_info(dev, "rumble setup success!\n");
 	return 0;
 }
